{"entries":[{"title":"Untitled","url":"/accessible-typography/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Designing for low-vision readers Designing for low-vision readers Designing for low-vision readers Most accessibility advice about typography stops at \"use 16px and good contrast.\" That's a start, but it leaves several decisions on the table that materially affect users with low vision. Three defaults worth changing Line-height of 1.5 — WCAG 1.4.12 calls for at least 1.5x the font size. Most CMS defaults sit around 1.2-1.3. Maximum line length of 80 characters — beyond that, eye-tracking studies show fatigue rises sharply for users with reduced central vision. Avoid pure white — #fff on #000 produces glare that low-vision users describe as \"stinging.\" A near-white like #fafafa removes the effect without hurting contrast ratios. What we ship by default Our base stylesheet uses 1.7 line-height (leaving room for users to bump to 2 in their reader settings without things breaking), max-width: 65ch on body copy, and oklch(99% 0 0) for the page background. None of these costs a single conversion.","headings":["Designing for low-vision readers","Designing for low-vision readers","Three defaults worth changing","What we ship by default"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/contact/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Contact Contact Contact Reach us at hello@threshold.press. We'd love to hear from you.","headings":["Contact","Contact"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/eaa-checklist/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>EAA enforcement: a 12-point pre-launch checklist EAA enforcement: a 12-point pre-launch checklist EAA enforcement: a 12-point pre-launch checklist The 12 checks we run before sign-off The European Accessibility Act covers any commercial site that serves EU consumers. For most of our clients, the practical scope is WCAG 2.1 AA — and that maps to twelve recurring failure points we see across audits. Contrast ratio ≥ 4.5:1 for body text, 3:1 for large text Every <img> has a meaningful alt (or alt=\"\" if decorative, with role=\"presentation\") Heading hierarchy descends without skipping levels Focus order matches DOM order on tab Focus indicator is visible (not the browser default outline removed) Touch targets ≥ 44×44 CSS pixels (WCAG 2.5.5) Form fields have associated <label> elements Error messages are programmatically linked to the offending field via aria-describedby Page is operable without a mouse (test with Tab + Enter only) Page is readable at 200% zoom without horizontal scroll <html lang="\&quot;...\&quot;"> is set Motion respects prefers-reduced-motion What we don't bother with ARIA roles you can express with native HTML. We've never had a single audit issue from \"should have used role='button'\" — only from \"should have used <button> instead of <div role="button">.\"","headings":["EAA enforcement: a 12-point pre-launch checklist","EAA enforcement: a 12-point pre-launch checklist","The 12 checks we run before sign-off","What we don't bother with"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文ThresholdPractical writing on accessibility, WCAG, and inclusive design — for product teams shipping into the EU.Get Started Learn MoreA journal for teams that ship accessible productsThreshold is a small, independent journal for product designers, engineers, and accessibility leads working on real systems. We write up the patterns that work, the audit traps that don't, and the regulatory shifts that change what \"compliant\" means in practice.No newsletters with five tracking pixels. No sponsored \"deep dives\". No takes on whether AI will replace designers. Just the writing we wish existed when we started doing this work.LatestEAA enforcement: a 12-point pre-launch checklist — the audit list we run before signing off any site that serves EU consumersWCAG 2.2: what actually changed for builders — nine new criteria, three of them affect every form on the webDesigning for low-vision readers — type scale, line-height, and contrast defaults that go further than \"use 16px\"See all posts or browse by topic.What we coverWCAG & EAA — the criteria, the audit process, the failure modesInclusive design patterns — typography, motion, colour, focusTooling — what passes (and what flatters) automated auditsCase writeups — what we tried, what worked, what didn'tSubscribeRSS feed — for any reader (we like NetNewsWire and Feedbin)Atom feed — for the older crowdNo email list. No tracking. Just the feeds.","headings":["Threshold","A journal for teams that ship accessible products","Latest","What we cover","Subscribe"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/posts/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Posts Posts All posts Designing for low-vision readers — How type scale, line-height, and contrast affect users with low vision — with practical defaults you can ship today. EAA enforcement: a 12-point pre-launch checklist — The European Accessibility Act lands in June 2025. Here's the audit list we run on every site before signing it off. WCAG 2.2: what actually changed for builders — Nine new criteria, three of them affect every form on the web. A practical translation from spec language to ship-it advice. Subscribe to the RSS feed or Atom feed to follow new posts.","headings":["Posts","All posts"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/privacy/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Privacy Privacy Privacy This site does not collect personal data, set cookies, or track visitors.","headings":["Privacy","Privacy"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/tags/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Tags Tags Browse by tag Browse all content organised by topic. Select a tag to discover related pages. Featured Tags (32) EAA (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: EAA enforcement: a 12-point pre-launch checklist - The European Accessibility Act lands in June 2025. Here's the audit list we run on every site before signing it off. WCAG (3 Posts) April 16, 2026: Designing for low-vision readers - How type scale, line-height, and contrast affect users with low vision — with practical defaults you can ship today. April 16, 2026: EAA enforcement: a 12-point pre-launch checklist - The European Accessibility Act lands in June 2025. Here's the audit list we run on every site before signing it off. April 16, 2026: WCAG 2.2: what actually changed for builders - Nine new criteria, three of them affect every form on the web. A practical translation from spec language to ship-it advice. Accessibility (3 Posts) April 16, 2026: Designing for low-vision readers - How type scale, line-height, and contrast affect users with low vision — with practical defaults you can ship today. April 16, 2026: EAA enforcement: a 12-point pre-launch checklist - The European Accessibility Act lands in June 2025. Here's the audit list we run on every site before signing it off. April 16, 2026: WCAG 2.2: what actually changed for builders - Nine new criteria, three of them affect every form on the web. A practical translation from spec language to ship-it advice. Blog (7 Posts) April 16, 2026: Page Not Found - Page Not Found for Threshold April 16, 2026: Contact - Contact for Threshold April 16, 2026: Threshold - An accessibility journal: WCAG, EAA, and inclusive design writing for product teams. April 16, 2026: Offline - Offline for Threshold April 16, 2026: Posts - Articles on accessibility, EAA compliance, and inclusive design. April 16, 2026: Privacy - Privacy for Threshold April 16, 2026: Tags - Tags for Threshold Compliance (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: EAA enforcement: a 12-point pre-launch checklist - The European Accessibility Act lands in June 2025. Here's the audit list we run on every site before signing it off. Design (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: Designing for low-vision readers - How type scale, line-height, and contrast affect users with low vision — with practical defaults you can ship today. Example (7 Posts) April 16, 2026: Page Not Found - Page Not Found for Threshold April 16, 2026: Contact - Contact for Threshold April 16, 2026: Threshold - An accessibility journal: WCAG, EAA, and inclusive design writing for product teams. April 16, 2026: Offline - Offline for Threshold April 16, 2026: Posts - Articles on accessibility, EAA compliance, and inclusive design. April 16, 2026: Privacy - Privacy for Threshold April 16, 2026: Tags - Tags for Threshold Legal (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: EAA enforcement: a 12-point pre-launch checklist - The European Accessibility Act lands in June 2025. Here's the audit list we run on every site before signing it off. Ssg (7 Posts) April 16, 2026: Page Not Found - Page Not Found for Threshold April 16, 2026: Contact - Contact for Threshold April 16, 2026: Threshold - An accessibility journal: WCAG, EAA, and inclusive design writing for product teams. April 16, 2026: Offline - Offline for Threshold April 16, 2026: Posts - Articles on accessibility, EAA compliance, and inclusive design. April 16, 2026: Privacy - Privacy for Threshold April 16, 2026: Tags - Tags for Threshold Typography (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: Designing for low-vision readers - How type scale, line-height, and contrast affect users with low vision — with practical defaults you can ship today.","headings":["Tags","Browse by tag","Featured Tags (32)","EAA (1 Posts)","WCAG (3 Posts)","Accessibility (3 Posts)","Blog (7 Posts)","Compliance (1 Posts)","Design (1 Posts)","Example (7 Posts)","Legal (1 Posts)","Ssg (7 Posts)","Typography (1 Posts)"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/wcag-2-1-vs-2-2/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>WCAG 2.2: what actually changed for builders WCAG 2.2: what actually changed for builders WCAG 2.2: what actually changed for builders The nine new criteria, one paragraph each WCAG 2.2 added nine success criteria. Most are tightenings of existing rules; three are genuinely new constraints. The three that change daily work 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (AA) — sticky headers can no longer cover the keyboard focus indicator. If your nav is sticky and your focus ring is on the second visible item, the spec wants the page to scroll until the focused element is visible. 2.5.7 Dragging Movements (AA) — anything you can do by dragging must also be doable with a single tap (or click). Card-sort interfaces, image carousels, drag-to-dismiss banners — all need keyboard equivalents. 3.3.7 Redundant Entry (A) — checkout flows can no longer ask the user to re-type information they've already entered (billing address re-entered as shipping, etc.). Auto-fill is acceptable; re-typing is not. What stayed the same Colour contrast ratios. Heading structure rules. Image alt requirements. The fundamentals of WCAG 2.1 AA carry over unchanged — 2.2 is additive, not a rewrite.","headings":["WCAG 2.2: what actually changed for builders","WCAG 2.2: what actually changed for builders","The nine new criteria, one paragraph each","The three that change daily work","What stayed the same"]}]}
